<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Launcher shortcuts: long-press the app icon to start a scan without passing through Home.

  ## Why only two

  The app has three ways in — barcode, nutrition label, and search — and only the two *scanners* are
  here. Search is not a peer of the other two in this product: it is reached as a recovery route when
  a lookup fails, never as the ordinary way to start, so promoting it to the launcher would advertise
  it as a main entry point and misstate what the app is for. The two scanners are what someone
  standing in a kitchen with the packet already in their hand actually wants.

  ## Why static rather than dynamic

  These never change, so there is nothing for the app to keep in step at runtime: a static list is
  published by the manifest at install time and cannot drift out of date with the app's state, and it
  needs no code running on the launch path to maintain it.

  ## The intent

  Each carries a distinct action string that `MainActivity` reads once and turns into a single
  navigation.

  **The intent is resolved by an intent-filter, not by naming a component here**, and that is a
  correctness requirement rather than a style choice. A shortcut resource cannot name this app's
  package portably: `${applicationId}` is a *manifest* placeholder and ships as literal text from a
  resource XML, while `@string/...` is stored by the framework as an unresolved resource reference —
  both were tried and both were confirmed broken by dumping the built APK and `dumpsys shortcut`, and
  both produce shortcuts that silently do nothing because they point at no installed package. The
  debug build's `.debug` applicationId suffix is what makes a hardcoded package wrong too. So each
  action is declared as an intent-filter on MainActivity (see AndroidManifest.xml) and matched by
  action alone, which is suffix-independent and resolves within this app by construction.

  Behaviour when the app has never been opened is handled in code, not here: see `StartupDestination`,
  which holds a shortcut until onboarding has been seen rather than dropping a first-time user
  straight into a camera.
-->
<shortcuts xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <shortcut
        android:shortcutId="scan_barcode"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_shortcut_barcode"
        android:shortcutShortLabel="@string/shortcut_scan_barcode_short"
        android:shortcutLongLabel="@string/shortcut_scan_barcode_long">
        <intent
            android:action="app.justthecarbs.action.SCAN_BARCODE" />
    </shortcut>

    <shortcut
        android:shortcutId="scan_label"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_shortcut_label"
        android:shortcutShortLabel="@string/shortcut_scan_label_short"
        android:shortcutLongLabel="@string/shortcut_scan_label_long">
        <intent
            android:action="app.justthecarbs.action.SCAN_LABEL" />
    </shortcut>

</shortcuts>
